The previous round's dispatch told you to preserve the `recordImport` call on the unchanged-content path of `AutoImportIfNewer`. That instruction was wrong, and this round corrects it — the behavior it preserved had a rationale ("refreshes the import timestamp so a mtime-only change stops looking new") that this very work item abolishes, and preserving it created a defect.

## Scope — exactly this fix, nothing else

**`internal/autoimport/autoimport.go:105`, the `StatusFresh` branch.** `currentHash` is the hash of the bytes `os.ReadFile` returned at line 76. `ContentState` reaches its Fresh verdict by independently re-reading and re-hashing the file (`jsonlpub.sampleState` → `HashFile`). So Fresh can be true because the *new* bytes match committed-or-pending while `currentHash` matches nothing. Recording that orphan hash sets `jsonl_content_hash` to a value describing content the database neither holds nor has on disk, and `clearPending` destroys the pending record that described the real content — after which every `ContentState` returns `StatusDiverged`, `cmd/bd/staleness.go:48` prints "Database out of sync with JSONL" on a healthy repo, and `jsonlpub.Publish` refuses every export with `ErrDiverged`. That is precisely the failure this work item exists to eliminate. It is new in commit `beee58774`: before it, the branch condition was `currentHash == lastHash`, so the hash compared and the hash recorded always described the same bytes.

Fix: delete the `recordImport` call from the `StatusFresh` branch so it only logs and returns, matching the sibling CLI path in `cmd/bd/autoflush.go`, whose Fresh branch already returns without recording. Remove the now-false comment above it.

This is safe because nothing in non-test code reads `last_import_time` — verify that yourself with a grep and report the result. Its only non-test appearances are the key constant at `internal/jsonlpub/jsonlpub.go:45`, the multi-repo writer at `cmd/bd/daemon_sync.go:282`, and comments. Staleness is content-based now, so the timestamp-refresh rationale is vestigial, and `contentStateLocked` already promotes a lingering pending hash at the next locked event.

Do not change the `StatusNoFile`, `StatusDiverged`, or `StatusNoMetadata` branches, the error-direction mapping, or the `recordImport` call on the post-import path (`internal/autoimport/autoimport.go:140`), which correctly records bytes it actually parsed.

Add a regression test pinning the defect: a file whose bytes differ from the ones the caller read, in a state where `ContentState` returns Fresh, must leave `jsonl_content_hash` and `jsonl_pending_hash` untouched. Verify the test fails on the pre-fix tree and record that output, as the previous rounds did (restore the old code with the Edit tool, never `git checkout`).

Do not make any other change. Do not add guards, tests, or refactors beyond the above.

